Expose AddPeer, RemovePeer and a socket protector to the mobile bindings

The mobile wrapper takes its peers from the config at start and has no way to
change them while the node runs, though Core.AddPeer and Core.RemovePeer do
exactly that. A host that finds better peers after the tunnel is up, or whose
peers have all gone dead, needs both, and a way to keep a new link out of the
tunnel (see core.SocketProtector).

  mobile.AddPeer(uri)       dials a peer on the running node and keeps redialling
                            it, like an entry of Peers in the config
  mobile.RemovePeer(uri)    closes the link and stops redialling; a peer that is
                            not configured is an error
  mobile.Protector          implemented by the host; Protect(fd) is
                            VpnService.protect on Android
  mobile.SetProtector(p)    routes every outbound peer socket through p; call it
                            before StartJSON

The tests start two nodes on loopback, with multicast off so that only the peer
under test can link them: AddPeer brings a link up, RemovePeer takes it down for
good, and a peer dial asks the protector about its socket.

